v4: canon results are XNEWVEC (plain malloc, intentionally unbounded —
cc1 lifetime) rather than ggc_alloc_atomic: the rewrite must not perturb
the GGC allocation stream, because gcc's var-tracking output
(.debug_loclists representative choice among equivalent location
expressions) is sensitive to GGC allocation order — the ggc-allocating
v3 of this patch flipped location-list entries in previously
byte-matching CUs.

v5: NIX_DEBUG_CANON_PREFIX_MAP accepts multiple colon-separated pairs
("OLD=NEW[:OLD=NEW...]", first match wins; single-pair behavior is
unchanged).  Needed because the depends tree must ALSO be canon-mapped
(store path -> /bitcoin/depends/<triple>) instead of -ffile-prefix-map:
gcc's own remap_filename ggc-allocates every fired rewrite, and a map
that fires where GUIX's environment fires none (their depends is at the
real /bitcoin, outside every map) flips var-tracking loclists in big
CUs — an IDENTITY map alone reproduces the flip (replay-verified on
armhf net_processing.cpp).

v6: the canon also covers the MACRO (__FILE__) remapping —
-ffile-prefix-map was doing double duty as debug AND macro map, so
dropping it from argv leaked raw store paths into .rodata via boost
assert macros (which changed RUNTIME binaries, not just .dbg).  Order
differs from the debug side: macro maps are tried FIRST and the canon
only rewrites what no map matched, because cmake spells its
-fmacro-prefix-map with the build-local source dir and that map must
keep winning for source files (it fires equivalently in the reference
environment); the canon covers only the depends remainder, which the
reference never maps.

